---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import FineTuneCostEstimator from '../../components/FineTuneCostEstimator.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Fine-Tuning Cost Estimator"
  description="Estimate the training and inference cost of fine-tuning GPT, Gemini, and other models on your own dataset."
>
  <FineTuneCostEstimator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Fine-tuning costs more than just training</h2>
  <p>
    Most people budget for the training run and forget that fine-tuned
    models often cost more per token at inference time than the base model.
    If you're running high volume, that inference markup can dwarf the
    one-time training cost.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use this estimator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your training dataset size in tokens.</li>
    <li>Enter how many epochs (passes over the data) you plan to run — 3 is a common default.</li>
    <li>Compare training cost and ongoing inference pricing across models.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>What's an epoch?</strong> One full pass through your training
    dataset. More epochs can improve results but also increases training
    cost linearly and risks overfitting on small datasets.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Is fine-tuning always worth it?</strong> Not always — for many
    use cases, prompt engineering or retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
    achieves similar results without training cost or inference markup.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
